hey David you've interviewed a lot of  extremists on the David Pakman show  there's now a debate about platforming  and a battle of ideas where is the line  between platforming Dangerous Ideas and  simply having a battle of ideas  excellent question  I've been engaged in a bunch of Twitter  discussions about this recently  including with people like Kyle kolinsky  of secular talk and others here's what  it comes down to responsible platforming  okay my approach to this question of  there's someone with an outrageous  horrible idea do you interview them  potentially giving them an opportunity  to spread their propaganda and to  convince more people of what they what  they believe but or do you ignore them  completely with the idea that their  ideas will go away and the answer is  like most things it depends okay when we  consider having an extremist on I'm  evaluating two basic things number one  are their ideas prominent enough that  they are worth refuting okay that's  number one so for example I've never  interviewed anyone on the program who  believes that to my knowledge I've never  interviewed anyone on the program about  the belief that Paul McCartney died and  was replaced by another left-handed  bassist who looks a lot like him and  sounds a lot like him when he sings it's  not a prominent enough thing and it's  not of consequence to the point where I  think it's even worth refuting but let's  say it were worth refuting then I go to  characteristic number two in my  decision-making process which is am I  going to be well enough equipped to do  the refuting okay and I don't do as good  a job of this as I would like but when I  bring on an extremist without rageous  ideas  I don't simply take the position of tell  me about what you believe oh really  that's interesting oh all Jews should  should self-immolate oh that that's very  interesting yeah and why do you think  that oh and though what kind of fire  starter should they be using no that's  not what I would consider responsible  platforming right I need to be prepared  to really challenge a challenge head-on  the liabilities of the underlying  philosophy the implications history  whatever I don't always do as good of a  job as I want to do with this but at  least that's my goal okay so for me the  line between the dangerous platforming  of ideas and engaging in a battle of  ideas is less about the person and the  ideas in a vacuum but it's a calculation  as to whether the person and bad ideas  are prominent enough to be worth  refuting and am I going to be positioned  well enough to refute those ideas if the  answer to either of those questions is  no then I don't do it because I think I  do risk providing a sort of propaganda  platform that could of run completely  counter to what I think are the goals of  my program other people would handle it  differently there are plenty of people  out there many of you know who they are  who invite horrible extremists with vile  and disgusting ideas with the idea that  they just want to hear from them they  want to hear what the ideas are they're  not there to provide counter points  they're not there to provide opposition  or even to suggest that these are  disgusting ideas dangerous ideas violent  ideas that's not me okay that's not what  I do  plenty of shows for people who like that  other approach but I don't think it's  particularly responsible so hopefully  that kind of gives you a sense of my  approach
 

